1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,000
This is part two.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:10,000
These are the real Selenium tests from the project, unchanged, now running in a visible Chrome window.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:18,000
I only added a short pause after every click and a red outline on the element about to be clicked, so you can follow.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Without those pauses, all five journeys take about twenty seconds.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Each test starts from a new guest and a clean session.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Journey one.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:40,000
The test opens the register page, types a name, a unique email and a password, clicks Create account, then waits until the name appears in the navigation bar.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:49,000
It asserts that the name is right, that the message says the account has been created, and that the browser is on the rooms page.

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Journey two.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The test registers a guest and logs out.

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Then it tries to log in with a wrong password and asserts that the error message appears and that the guest is still logged out.

12
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Then it logs in with the right password and asserts that the welcome back message appears.

13
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
This checks both the refusal and the success.

14
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Journey three, the core of the application.

15
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:26,000
A new guest searches rooms for two nights, books the first free room, and the test asserts the status is PENDING and the dates are correct.

16
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Then it clicks Confirm and waits for the status to change to CONFIRMED.

17
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
It also asserts that the confirm button disappears, so a booking cannot be confirmed twice.

18
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Journey four.

19
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
A guest books and confirms, then clicks Cancel.

20
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,000
A dialog asks for confirmation, and the test asserts that nothing is cancelled yet.

21
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,000
After accepting, the status becomes CANCELLED and the cancel button disappears.

22
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Finally the test opens My bookings and asserts that the row for this booking shows CANCELLED.

23
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
That is status tracking.

24
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Journey five protects the most important rule.

25
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
The first guest books a room and logs out.

26
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
A second guest registers and searches the same dates.

27
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:22,000
The test asserts that the room now shows Booked, and that there is no Book button for it.

28
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
So two guests can never hold the same room for the same nights.

29
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
That was all five journeys, with the real application and a real browser.

30
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
The run ends with five passed, as in the terminal part of this video.

31
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:49,000
This is what Jenkins runs in its Selenium stage, headless, every time someone pushes a commit, and a failure there stops the deployment.


